What is a Branch?

A branch is an independent line of development.

It allows developers to work on new features or bug fixes without affecting the main codebase.

Imagine the main branch is the production highway:

main
 │
 │
 │
 └────────────► Production

A developer creates a new feature:

main
 │
 ├──────── feature-login
 │
 └────────► Production

The feature branch is isolated until it's ready.

Why Do We Need Branches?

Without branches:

Developer A
        │
Developer B
        │
Developer C
        │
        ▼
main

Everyone changes the same branch, increasing the risk of broken builds and conflicts.

With branches:

main
 │
 ├── feature-login
 ├── feature-payment
 └── bugfix-auth

Each developer works independently.

Create a Branch

List branches:

git branch

Create:

git branch feature-login

Nothing changes yet—you've only created the branch.

Switch to a Branch

Older command:

git checkout feature-login

Modern command (recommended):

git switch feature-login
Create and Switch
git switch -c feature-login

Equivalent to:

git branch feature-login
git switch feature-login
Verify Current Branch
git branch

Example:

* feature-login
  main

The * indicates the active branch.

Branch Workflow
main
 │
 ├───── feature-login
 │         │
 │         │ Commit
 │         │ Commit
 │         ▼
 └──────── Merge
Commit on a Branch
git switch feature-login

git add .

git commit -m "Added login page"

Only feature-login contains these commits.

Merge a Branch

Switch back to main:

git switch main

Merge:

git merge feature-login

Result:

main
 │
 ├──── feature-login
 │
 ▼
Merged
Delete a Branch

After merging:

git branch -d feature-login

Force delete (use carefully):

git branch -D feature-login
Merge Conflict

A merge conflict occurs when two branches modify the same part of the same file.

Example:

main
print("Version A")
feature-login
print("Version B")

Git cannot decide which version to keep.

It reports:

CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict
Conflict Markers
<<<<<<< HEAD
Version A
=======
Version B
>>>>>>> feature-login

You manually edit the file:

print("Version B")

or combine both changes if appropriate.

Then:

git add app.py
git commit
Local Branch vs Remote Branch

Local:

Your Laptop
